**Not mandatory — but saves you from silent bugs.**

```java
// WITHOUT @FunctionalInterface — works, but no safety net:
interface TradeFilter {
    boolean test(Trade trade);
}
// TradeFilter f = trade -> trade.isActive(); // compiles — it IS a functional interface

// Later, someone adds a second method accidentally:
interface TradeFilter {
    boolean test(Trade trade);
    boolean testStrict(Trade trade);  // OOPS! Now has 2 abstract methods!
}
// TradeFilter f = trade -> trade.isActive(); // COMPILE ERROR! But error is HERE, not at the interface!
// Hard to trace back to the interface change.

// WITH @FunctionalInterface — compiler catches at the SOURCE:
@FunctionalInterface
interface TradeFilter {
    boolean test(Trade trade);
    boolean testStrict(Trade trade);  // COMPILE ERROR RIGHT HERE! "Multiple non-overriding abstract methods"
}
// The bug is caught at the interface definition, not at every lambda usage site.
```

**Extending another interface — rules.**

```java
// Parent with 1 abstract method:
interface Describable { String describe(); }

// Child extends + adds 1 abstract = 2 total → NOT a functional interface
// @FunctionalInterface
// interface DescribableTrade extends Describable {
//     void process();  // COMPILE ERROR with @FunctionalInterface — 2 abstract methods!
// }

// FIX: override parent's abstract as default in child
@FunctionalInterface
interface DescribableTrade extends Describable {
    void process();  // 1 abstract method (Recommended) 
    @Override
    default String describe() { return "Trade processor"; }  // parent abstract → default (Recommended) 
}
// DescribableTrade dt = () -> System.out.println("Processing trade");

// Extending a non-functional interface that has NO abstract methods:
interface Serializable { }  // marker interface — 0 abstract methods
@FunctionalInterface
interface SerializableTrade extends Serializable {
    void execute();  // 1 abstract method (Recommended) (0 from parent + 1 from this)
}
```

**Default + static methods — lambdas still work because they implement the ONE abstract method.**

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface FeeCalculator {
    BigDecimal calculate(Trade trade);  // THE abstract method — lambda implements THIS

    // Default: composition — does NOT break @FunctionalInterface
    default FeeCalculator withMinimum(BigDecimal min) {
        return trade -> {
            BigDecimal fee = this.calculate(trade);
            return fee.compareTo(min) < 0 ? min : fee;
        };
    }

    // Static: factory — does NOT break @FunctionalInterface
    static FeeCalculator flatFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return trade -> amount;  // lambda — always returns same fee
    }

    static FeeCalculator percentageFee(BigDecimal rate) {
        return trade -> trade.getAmount().multiply(rate);
    }
}

// Usage: lambda implements calculate(), default/static are bonuses
FeeCalculator brokerage = FeeCalculator.percentageFee(new BigDecimal("0.001"))
    .withMinimum(new BigDecimal("20"));  // minimum Rs.20 brokerage
BigDecimal fee = brokerage.calculate(trade);
```

